.PDXDOC 1
.NAME    mv
.VERSION 1.0.0
.SUMMARY move / rename one path onto another with atomic PdxFS TXN + audit + undo
.CAT     A/coreutils
.WAVE    R50
.DEFAULT_TOPIC synopsis

.TOPIC synopsis
.HEAD SYNOPSIS
mv [-v|--verbose] [-i] [--dry-run] <src> <dst>

Move (or rename) SRC onto DST inside a single PdxFS v1 transaction.
Success is atomic: at commit boundary the source dirent is gone and the
destination dirent points at the same inode. Failure at any stage aborts
the TXN, restoring the source and leaving no destination artifact.

.TOPIC description
.HEAD DESCRIPTION
mv handles four move shapes inside one dispatch entry
(Move::move_dispatch):

  same-dir        src_parent == dst_parent -- one dirent rewrite in the
                  shared parent, one TXN.

  cross-dir       src_parent != dst_parent, same device -- unlink at
                  src_parent + link at dst_parent, both threaded through
                  ONE TXN (still atomic).

  cross-device    src_parent and dst_parent live on different PdxFS
                  volumes -- degrades to cp+rm inside the same outer
                  TXN so the "atomic move" contract holds at commit
                  boundary. --verbose surfaces was_cross_device on
                  the diagnostic line.

  cross-user      dst_parent's owning KIND_USER tail differs from
                  src's inode tail -- Elevate::mv_elevate_cross_user
                  performs a best-effort broker hop to re-sign the
                  inode under the destination-user's key. A denied
                  elevate falls through to graceful degrade: the
                  inode keeps its old owner tail and a diagnostic
                  records the degrade.

Every successful mv emits ONE MoveRecord on the semantic pipe
(schema id 0xFFFFEB5000000001) carrying (magic, was_rename,
was_cross_device, was_cross_user, src_ptr, dst_ptr, src_inode,
src_parent, dst_parent, txn_handle) before any user-visible output
leaves the process (D3 audit-first). The move is also reported to
/system/audit/user-events/ via libpdx-audit's AuditClient lifecycle
(audit_begin / audit_record_output / audit_commit) before the TXN
commit; on the commit path Undo::mv_undo_write threads a PdxFS undo
record whose replay is `mv <dst> <src>` -- pdx-undo (R51) reads it
and reverses the op.

.TOPIC options
.HEAD OPTIONS
  -v, --verbose      on a successful commit, print one advisory line
                     per condition that fired: "mv: crossed device
                     (cp+rm fallback, not O(1))" if the cross-device
                     fallback ran, and/or "mv: crossed user (inode
                     kept old owner)" if the cross-user rebind
                     degraded. Prints nothing if neither condition
                     fired -- it does not echo `mv <src> <dst>`.

  -i                 parsed, no consumer yet -- the confirm prompt
                     needs a TTY line reader (KIND_TTY, shell.M4)
                     this tree does not have. The destination-clobber
                     guard this flag will gate (refuse-by-default
                     when dst already exists) is tracked separately
                     as mv.ENH-004 and does not yet exist either, so
                     `mv src dst` silently overwrites an existing
                     dst today.

      --dry-run      resolve both parents and the source inode
                     (parse + validate + resolve), then return
                     success without opening the TXN, without
                     emitting a MoveRecord, without journaling, and
                     without threading an undo record. Nothing is
                     written.

.TOPIC exit_codes
.HEAD EXIT CODES
Per I4 (design/tooling/plan.md), mv obeys the shared exit-code
convention plus the mv-specific band 0xFFFFEBxx (high two bytes
0xFFFFEB identify the mv layer to a downstream consumer):

   0  success -- MoveRecord emitted, TXN committed, undo record
                  threaded.
   1  reserved -- mv has no "query returned nothing" outcome.
   2  user error -- MV_ARG_* band (0xFFFFEB1x): unknown flag,
                  wrong positional count, malformed argv.
   3  system error -- MV_RN_* / MV_MV_* bands (0xFFFFEB2x /
                  0xFFFFEB3x): TXN open refused, unlink failed,
                  link failed, commit refused, cross-device
                  fallback failed.
   4  capability denied -- caller's InitCap sidecar missing one
                  of the six caps in caps.decl (KIND_USER /
                  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT / KIND_PDXFS_FILE write on
                  src-parent + dst-parent / KIND_PDXFS_TXN
                  invoke+mint / KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL invoke).
   5  signing / verification failure -- reserved; mv's manifest.
                  pdxsig is verified by pkg at install time, not
                  by mv itself.

Full sub-band table lives in `design/architecture.md` §5.

.TOPIC caps
.HEAD CAPABILITIES
mv declares six required caps in `caps.decl` (I6, refusable via
--no-cap:<name>):

  KIND_USER (self)                       identity root; mv reads
                                         the caller's KIND_USER tail
                                         before touching an inode.
  KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT (invoke)             baseline pipe substrate
                                         (R20b); MoveRecord[] emission
                                         via libpdx-semantic-pipe.
  KIND_PDXFS_FILE (write, <src-parent>)  write authority on the
                                         directory holding src.
  KIND_PDXFS_FILE (write, <dst-parent>)  write authority on the
                                         directory that will hold
                                         dst; same as src-parent for
                                         same-dir rename.
  KIND_PDXFS_TXN (invoke, mint)          transaction authority;
                                         invoke for query on an open
                                         TXN, mint to open a fresh
                                         one.
  KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL (invoke,          best-effort cross-user hop;
    svc.elevate-broker)                  a denied elevate falls
                                         through to graceful degrade.

At startup mv prints the requested cap set to stderr unless --quiet
(I6). --no-cap:<name> strips a cap; if the stripped cap is one of
the two KIND_PDXFS_FILE writes or KIND_PDXFS_TXN, mv exits 4
before any TXN is opened.

.TOPIC schemas
.HEAD OUTPUT SCHEMAS
mv emits ONE typed schema on its semantic pipe:

  MoveRecord[] (schema id 0xFFFFEB5000000001, record size 80 bytes,
  ten u64 fields at src/schema.pdx's fixed offsets)
    fields:
      magic           u64  MOVE_RECORD_MAGIC (schema id, offset 0)
      was_rename      u64  1 if src_parent == dst_parent
      was_cross_device u64 1 if the cross-device fallback fired
      was_cross_user  u64  1 if dst_parent's owner != src's owner
      src_ptr         u64  caller-owned pointer to the source path
      dst_ptr         u64  caller-owned pointer to the destination path
      src_inode       u64  the moved inode's PdxFS id
      src_parent      u64  source parent directory inode
      dst_parent      u64  destination parent directory inode
      txn_handle      u64  correlates the record to PdxFS TXN state

There is no `elevated` field -- the elevate hop's outcome is not
carried in MoveRecord at this release; a denied elevate is only
observable via the -v advisory or the graceful-degrade owner tail.

Downstream consumers filter, sort, or plot MoveRecord[] by any of
these fields via the D2 semantic-pipe convention -- no column
slicing.

.TOPIC audit
.HEAD AUDIT
Every successful mv reports itself to the user-events journal via
libpdx-audit before the TXN commit (D3 audit-first: an mv that
cannot log itself is refused). Audit::mv_audit_write_move drives
libpdx-audit's AuditClient three-call lifecycle -- audit_begin("mv"),
audit_record_output(audit_id, "MoveRecord@0.1", output_hash), then
audit_commit(audit_id, exit_code) -- which itself binds
`svc.audit-journal` and issues the sys_ipc_send wire libpdx-audit
owns. A non-zero return from any of the three calls aborts the TXN.

.TOPIC undo
.HEAD UNDO
On the commit path Undo::mv_undo_write threads a PdxFS v1 undo
record (magic 0xFFFFEB7000000001, replay opcode 0x6D76) whose fields
are the swap:

  replay_src  <-  dst
  replay_dst  <-  src

pdx-undo (R51) reads the record from the TXN's WAL tail and
invokes `mv <replay_src> <replay_dst>`, restoring the pre-move
state. Undo is best-effort at the write side -- an undo-write
failure does NOT abort the TXN (the audit journal at M3-002
still records the op so the operation is discoverable through
journal-x even without the WAL undo).

.TOPIC ergonomics
.HEAD ERGONOMICS (D5)
The five most common tasks mv serves, keystroke cost, failure
protections, and recovery path:

  1. rename in place        mv old.txt new.txt        11 keys
     protection: unknown-flag reject, empty argv reject.
     recovery:   pdx-undo restores old.txt.

  2. move into subdir       mv file.txt sub/          15 keys
     protection: dst_parent write cap enforced at exec;
                 caller sees exit 4 before any TXN opens if
                 sub/ is not writable.
     recovery:   pdx-undo restores file.txt at repo root.

  3. cross-volume move      mv file /mnt/other/       18 keys
     protection: cross-device fallback (cp+rm) still atomic
                 at commit boundary; TXN abort restores src.
     recovery:   pdx-undo restores file at original path.

  4. cross-user handoff     mv file ~otheruser/inbox/ 25 keys
     protection: best-effort elevate hop re-signs the inode;
                 a denied elevate falls through to graceful
                 degrade (verbose diagnostic marks it).
     recovery:   pdx-undo replays with elevate hop reversed.

  5. dry-run preview        mv --dry-run a b          16 keys
     protection: no TXN opens, no journal, no MoveRecord --
                 pure validation surface.
     recovery:   none required; nothing was done.

.TOPIC posix
.HEAD DIFFERENCES FROM POSIX mv
The classic POSIX mv is a wrapper around rename(2) with a cp+rm
fallback for cross-device moves. PaideiaOS mv differs on three
axes:

  atomicity        POSIX rename is atomic for same-dir + cross-dir
                   on same device; cross-device is best-effort.
                   PaideiaOS mv is atomic in all four cases via
                   the single TXN + audit-first + undo-record
                   discipline.

  auditability     POSIX mv is silent about the operation once
                   it returns. PaideiaOS mv journals a MoveRecord
                   to /system/audit/user-events/ BEFORE the
                   commit; journal-x (R51) surfaces it.

  reversibility    POSIX mv is destructive at rename boundary --
                   the source dirent is gone with no artifact.
                   PaideiaOS mv threads a WAL undo record so
                   pdx-undo replays the reverse mv.

POSIX -f (force) has no equivalent here -- mv never prompts today
(-i has no consumer; see .TOPIC options), so every mv already
behaves as if -f were implied. POSIX -n (no-clobber) has no
equivalent either: mv does not check whether dst exists before
linking over it, so an existing dst is silently overwritten with
no record of what was destroyed (mv.ENH-004 tracks the guard;
mv.ENH-005 tracks wiring it to -i's prompt once KIND_TTY lands).
POSIX -u (only-if-newer) is deferred to M6+ (no user demand at
first-slice; a future refinement).

